  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front passing through the region will \ntrigger additional showers and thunderstorms capable of heavy \nrain leading to flash flooding. There is also a risk for strong \nto severe thunderstorms.\n\nThe cold front continues to work its way through eastern\nPennsylvania, and latest surface obs show a noted drop in dew\npoints. Ahead of the cold front, dew points are well in the 70s,\nbut behind the front, dew points are generally in the low to mid\n60s. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms continue to fire up ahead of the\nfront, and due to those high dew points and PWATs in excess of 2\ninches, heavy rain continue to develop. This has resulted in\nflash flooding earlier today, and heavy rain is continuing to\nresult in additional flash flood and flood warnings. Will go\nahead and cancel the Flood Watch that was in effect for portions\nof northern New Jersey and southeast Pennsylvania where the rain\nhas ended, but the Flood Watch remains in effect elsewhere\nthrough this evening. The Weather Prediction Center has New\nJersey and portions of southeast Pennsylvania in a Slight Risk\nfor Excessive Rainfall (2 out of 4) for the rest of today.\n\nThe severe risk remains uncertain, though breaks in the clouds\nwill allow temperatures to warm into the lower 80s or so. SBCAPE\nvalues are upwards of 1500 to 2000 J/kg. There is also 40 kt of\nshear that will pass through the region with the front. As a\nresult, any severe storms will be isolated, but still possible.\nImportant to note that for areas where the ground becomes\nsaturated due to heavy rain, it will not take severe level gusts\nto bring down trees.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Low pressure south of the area will push a warm\nfront north through the region Tuesday night through Wednesday.\n\nThe cold front works its way through the region tonight, then\nwashes out over the area on Tuesday. High pressure builds in\nfrom the north and west on Tuesday before departing Tuesday\nnight. A pleasant day on tap for Tuesday with highs in the low\nto mid 80s and dew points generally in the low 60s.\n\nA warm front then lifts north through the region Tuesday night,\nallowing showers and isolated thunderstorms to develop late\nTuesday night and into Wednesday morning. Low pressure\ndeveloping over the Southeast lifts north along the Eastern\nSeaboard, but there is considerable uncertainty as to its track.\n12Z GFS takes it out to sea with minimal impacts to the area,\nwhile the 12Z ECMWF as it inland across Pennsylvania, where the\nheaviest rain will fall, and the 12Z NAM brings it right over\nthe I-95 corridor. Heavy rain will result in flash flooding, but\nit is uncertain as to where that heavy rain will fall. The\nWeather Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk for Excessive\nRainfall, which is a 1 out of 4. For now, will follow the NBM\nand carry high chance to low end likely PoPs for most of the\nregion. There is the potential for another 1/2 inch to 1 inch of\nrain Wednesday afternoon and evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...For the later half of the week, we will have a \nhot, humid, and at times stormy pattern in place.\n\nExpect a prolonged period of southwesterly flow as our region\nwill be in between a Bermuda High well to our east, and a mid\nlevel long wave trough that will be slowly sliding east over the\nnorthern tier of the U.S./southern Canada. As such, heat and\nhumidity will be building throughout the later half of the week.\nAt this point, Friday and Saturday appear to be the warmest\ndays, with highs ranging from the upper 70s to near 90. That in\nand of itself is pretty close to normal. However, with dewpoints\nin the mid 70s possible, we could get heat index values at or\nabove 100 degrees for much of the area. All that being said,\nthis still doesn't look nearly as impactful an extreme heat\nsetup as what we saw earlier this summer during the first week\nof July. \n\nThe other aspect of this are the daily storm chances, primarily\nconfined to the afternoon and evening with a typical diurnal\npattern. Given the hot and humid conditions that will be in\nplace, we can't rule out the more common thunderstorm hazards of\ndamaging winds and heavy rain/flooding, but in general, I don't\nsee any one day that has an especially high threat of any of\nthese at this time. If an embedded short wave trough crosses our\nregion as the larger trough axis approaches, that could increase\nthe risk, particularly for severe threats, but trying to time\nthat out at this point would be very difficult, given that we\nare still seeing some differences between the models with the\nlarger pattern.",
